//! English explanations of Interfaces › Wi-Fi Sniffer (G1.30j), scope `wifi_sniffer`,
//! sorted by key.

/// Explanations: (key, Markdown).
pub(super) const HELP: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("menu.wifi-sniffer", "## What it does\nPuts a Wi-Fi radio in monitor mode. The **Snooper** shows who uses the air, per network and per station: airtime, frames, retries, rate, signal and notes (far and slow, deauth with the reason, handshake, hidden network revealed, a twin of a network). The **Capture** records the 802.11 frames into a pcap (radiotap) for Wireshark. Like RouterOS 7's `/interface/wifi/sniffer` and the legacy snooper.\n\nIt never takes a radio off its network: on an enabled radio the monitor sits beside the station, on its channel."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.airtime", "## Airtime\n\nThe share of time the channel was busy with this network's or station's frames, over the time the radio listened to that channel. **Estimated**: each frame's duration comes from its size and rate (preamble + bits), without the gaps between frames or the back-off; the real channel is busier than this.\n\nFor a station it counts what it sends **and** what the access point sends to it. A slow station (low rate) takes much airtime for little traffic and delays everyone else."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.bssid", "## BSSID\n\nThe access point's address (of each network it announces). As a capture filter: only that network's frames. Empty = all."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.capture", "## Capture\n\nRecords whole 802.11 frames into a pcap file with a radiotap header (link type 127): Wireshark shows each frame's signal, channel, rate and retry mark. The filter picks what goes into the file and the live list; the Snooper's table still counts everything.\n\nThe file stays in the router's memory until downloaded (Download .pcap); it stops at its size limit, but the capture goes on until its duration."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.channel", "## Channel\n\nThe channel the radio listens on: the number (1 to 14 on 2.4 GHz, 36 to 177 on 5 GHz) or the frequency in MHz (6 GHz by MHz only). Exclusive radio only (interface disabled); on an enabled radio the monitor hears the station's channel.\n\nEmpty: the first channel the radio allows in the configured country."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.channel_time", "## Time per channel\n\nHow long the radio stays on each channel while hopping, in ms (50 to 10000; default 200, as RouterOS' snooper `channel-time`). Longer sees more of each channel; shorter goes round faster."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.channels", "## Channels\n\nThe channels to go round while hopping, comma separated: `1,6,11,36,44`. Empty: every channel the radio allows in the configured country."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.duration", "## Duration\n\nHow long the sniffer runs, in seconds (1 to 86400; default 300). It ends by itself: a forgotten monitor does not keep the radio. It also stops if nobody looks at the window for 60 s."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.eapol", "## Handshake only (EAPOL)\n\nKeeps only the EAPOL frames (the 4-way handshake of whoever joins the network) and one beacon per network (its name). That is what Wireshark needs to decrypt **your** network's traffic: in Preferences › Protocols › IEEE 802.11, turn on Enable decryption and add a `wpa-pwd` key `passphrase:SSID`.\n\nThe handshake is on the air only when a station joins (or comes back); the Snooper's table says when it was caught whole (the handshake 4/4 chip)."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.file_limit", "## File limit\n\nThe largest the pcap gets, in KiB (1 to 65536; default 16384 = 16 MiB). There the file stops growing and later frames count as **past the limit**; the Snooper goes on. At most two sniffers are kept at once."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.frame_types", "## Frame types\n\n**mgmt**: management (beacon, probe, authentication, association, deauth). **control**: control (ACK, RTS/CTS, Block Ack). **data**: data (ciphered, except on an open network and EAPOL). None ticked = all."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.frames", "## Frames\n\nHow many frames were heard from this network or station: what it sent and, for a station, what the access point sent to it."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.hop", "## Hop\n\nThe radio goes round the channels of the list, a while on each, and the table gathers what it heard on all of them. Exclusive radio only (interface disabled). Each channel is heard only on its turn: airtime is over each channel's listened time."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.interface", "## Interface\n\nThe radio that goes into monitor mode. The sniffer makes a monitor interface beside it (`wifi1mon`, gone at the end) and reads the frames with their radiotap header.\n\n**Enabled radio:** the station stays connected; the monitor hears its channel only. **Disabled radio:** the sniffer has the radio and picks the channel and width, and may hop."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.mac", "## MAC\n\nCapture filter: only the frames from or to this address (in any of the three address fields). Empty = all."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.mode", "## Mode\n\n**beside the station**: the radio is enabled and stays on its network; the monitor hears the station's channel. The sniffer never takes a radio off its network, which would cut the management that comes through it.\n\n**exclusive**: the interface is disabled and the sniffer has the radio. To choose the channel of the radio this session comes through, enter **Safe Mode** and disable the interface: if the connection drops, Safe Mode enables the radio again and the sniffer stops at once."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.network", "## Network / station\n\nOne row per network (the name and the access point's BSSID, in a chip coloured by the address) and, under it, one per station on it (with ·). At the end, the stations on no network (looking for one).\n\nClick a row to read its notes in full under the table."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.notes", "## Notes\n\nWhat the router notices in the air:\n\n- **far, slow**: many retries at a low rate or with a weak signal; the station takes everyone's airtime.\n- **deauth ×N**: how many times it was thrown out, with the reason (an 802.11 code); **deauth flood**: ten or more in one second, an attack or a broken AP.\n- **handshake 4/4**: the four EAPOL messages were caught: Wireshark decrypts this station with the passphrase.\n- **hidden, revealed**: the network keeps its name out of the beacon, but a probe response or an association gave it away.\n- **evil twin?**: another access point with the same name and weaker security (open). **same SSID, other security / other vendor**: worth a look."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.rate", "## Rate\n\nThe rate of the latest data frames, in Mb/s, with the standard (HT = Wi-Fi 4, VHT = Wi-Fi 5, HE = Wi-Fi 6), the MCS, the streams (SS) and the width. For a station, the one the access point uses for it. Computed from the radiotap header with the 802.11 tables."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.retry", "## Retries\n\nThe share of frames that went with the retry mark (the first send was not acknowledged). Above ~10 % the link is poor (weak signal, interference, a busy channel); above 20 % at a low rate it becomes the **far, slow** note."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.security", "## Security\n\nWhat the beacon announces: `open`, `owe` (open, ciphered), `wpa2`, `wpa3`, `wpa2-wpa3` (transition), `enterprise` (802.1X) or `legacy` (WEP/old WPA, broken). Open and legacy in amber."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.signal", "## Signal\n\nThe average signal of the frames the network or station sent, in dBm, as this router's radio heard them. Above -60 is good; below -75, weak."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.snooper", "## Snooper\n\nShows who uses the air: each network and, in it, each station, with airtime, frames, retries, rate, signal and notes; on top, each channel's airtime. It counts, it records no frames (for that, the Capture tab, which fills this table too)."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.ssid", "## SSID\n\nCapture filter: only the networks of this name (known from their beacons) and the probes for it. Empty = all."),
    ("wifi_sniffer.width", "## Width\n\nHow many MHz the radio listens to around each channel: 20, 40, 80 or 160, in the block the channel plan defines. On 20 MHz the radio does not decode an 80 MHz network's data (only its beacons, sent on 20): use the width of the network you want to see. Exclusive radio only."),
];
